## Proof

**Claim:** Yes. For any separable Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}$ and any closed subspace $U \subset \mathcal{H}$, there exists an orthonormal basis $\{e_i\}_{i=1}^{\infty}$ of $\mathcal{H}$ such that a subsequence of the $e_i$'s spans (i.e., has closed linear span equal to) $U$.

**Construction.** Since $\mathcal{H}$ is separable, every closed subspace of $\mathcal{H}$ is separable (a closed subspace of a separable metric space is separable). In particular $U$ is separable, so it admits a countable (finite or countably infinite) orthonormal basis $\{u_j\}_{j \in J}$, where $J \subseteq \mathbb{N}$.

The orthogonal complement $U^\perp$ is also a closed subspace of the separable Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}$, hence separable, and admits a countable orthonormal basis $\{f_k\}_{k \in K}$, where $K \subseteq \mathbb{N}$.

Since $\mathcal{H} = U \oplus U^\perp$, the union $\{u_j\}_{j \in J} \cup \{f_k\}_{k \in K}$ is a complete orthonormal system in $\mathcal{H}$. Because $\mathcal{H}$ is infinite-dimensional (the problem indexes the basis as $i = 1, 2, \dots$), at least one of $U, U^\perp$ is infinite-dimensional; in any case the union is countably infinite, so it can be enumerated as a sequence $\{e_i\}_{i=1}^{\infty}$.

**Ensuring the subsequence property.** We enumerate so that the $u_j$'s appear as a subsequence. Concretely, interleave: list $u_1, f_1, u_2, f_2, u_3, f_3, \dots$ if both $U$ and $U^\perp$ are infinite-dimensional. If $U$ is finite-dimensional (say $\dim U = m$) and $U^\perp$ is infinite-dimensional, list $u_1, \dots, u_m, f_1, f_2, f_3, \dots$; here $\{u_1, \dots, u_m\} = \{e_1, \dots, e_m\}$ is a (finite) subsequence whose span is $U$. If $U^\perp$ is finite-dimensional and $U$ is infinite-dimensional, list $f_1, \dots, f_n, u_1, u_2, u_3, \dots$; then $\{u_j\} = \{e_{n+1}, e_{n+2}, \dots\}$ is a subsequence whose closed linear span is $U$. If both are infinite-dimensional, the interleaving places the $u_j$'s at the odd indices $\{e_1, e_3, e_5, \dots\}$, a subsequence.

In every case, the $u_j$'s form a subsequence of $\{e_i\}$, and by construction $\overline{\operatorname{span}}\{u_j\} = U$.

**Verification that $\{e_i\}$ is an orthonormal basis.** Each $e_i$ is a unit vector; vectors from $\{u_j\}$ are mutually orthonormal, vectors from $\{f_k\}$ are mutually orthonormal, and every $u_j \perp f_k$ (since $\{u_j\} \subset U$ and $\{f_k\} \subset U^\perp$). Hence $\{e_i\}$ is orthonormal. Completeness: any $x \in \mathcal{H}$ decomposes uniquely as $x = P_U x + P_{U^\perp} x$ with $P_U x \in U = \overline{\operatorname{span}}\{u_j\}$ and $P_{U^\perp} x \in U^\perp = \overline{\operatorname{span}}\{f_k\}$, so $x$ lies in the closed linear span of $\{e_i\} = \{u_j\} \cup \{f_k\}$. Thus $\{e_i\}$ is a complete orthonormal system, i.e., an orthonormal basis.

**Conclusion.** Such a basis always exists: take orthonormal bases of $U$ and $U^\perp$ separately and enumerate their union so that the basis of $U$ appears as a subsequence.

### PROOF COMPLETE
